<h2>Design a Carnival Game (optional)</h2><p>Original warm-up: notice/wonder about carnival games, rules, fairness and how to win.</p>
<h3>Original design steps</h3><ol><li>Group of four designs its own game from physical classroom materials.</li><li>Describe original RULES and how the game is WON.</li><li>TEST original game <b>at least once</b> with players.</li><li>REDESIGN with <b>at least two</b> of four source mathematics elements below.</li><li><b>If time</b>, play revised game; optional.</li></ol>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source math element</th><th>Teacher observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>length or distance measurement in inches</td><td>Evidence of use in revised game: _______</td></tr><tr><td>time that has passed</td><td>Evidence of use in revised game: _______</td></tr><tr><td>multiplication and division within 100</td><td>Evidence of use in revised game: _______</td></tr><tr><td>addition and subtraction within 1,000</td><td>Evidence of use in revised game: _______</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Show the thinking</h3><p>Explain to someone outside the group how the game works, how to win, and what changed. Multiple valid designs and scores; teacher reviews real play, mathematical constraints, fairness and clarity. Additional material or a second day allowed by teacher.</p><p>Source student task PDF and publisher visuals not pixel-reviewed; no publisher cooldown reproduced.</p></section>